+# Textbook Manipulable Geometry Authoring v1
+
+Create a complete, original HTML 3D observation activity for the exact frozen textbook objective. Render actual geometry and let the learner make meaningful changes to objects. Keep the conceptual depth, vocabulary and operations within the frozen grade and scope. Do not copy unrelated physics, geometry terminology or measurement formulas into a simple observation task.
+
+{{snippet:textbook-activity-contract}}
+
+## Real geometry and meaningful teaching states
+
+- Use WebGL with real vertex/mesh geometry, camera projection, lighting and material contrast. Three.js is suitable when its actual dependency is available; a custom WebGL implementation is also valid. CSS perspective, flat transformed cards, a prerendered model picture or a canvas that only draws text are not 3D geometry.
+- Define at least three objective-relevant teaching states when the frozen design calls for exploration: observe/select an object, move/orient it toward a meaningful destination, and compare its changed placement or appearance with a reference. Use the design's own states and concepts rather than inventing a different lesson.
+- Model transformations must update real position/orientation and scene rendering. A camera preset alone does not complete an object task. A rotation changes actual geometry orientation and its supported floor placement, not only a label.
+- A wrong object, invalid placement or incomplete manipulation has a specific visible reason and fixed teacher cue, with a safe retry. Never reveal an answer before the learner acts or requests a demonstration. Continue is explicit after feedback.
+- Show recognizable life-object references and distinguishable model choices, with original SVG/image buttons where selection needs them. For younger children, an object name alone is insufficient. Keep actual models visible with contrast against the floor/background and enough light to perceive their form.
+
+## Complete renderer and viewport behavior
+
+Initialize the renderer, scene, camera, real geometries, materials and lights before enabling manipulation. Catch initialization/dependency failures and show a genuine 48-pixel retry button with an original SVG icon and a declared literal error cue. Do not leave a spinning/loading placeholder or silently substitute fake 3D. Success hides the retry/loading UI.
+
+Use a ResizeObserver on the actual canvas/container, not only a window resize listener:
+
+```javascript
+const resizeObserver = new ResizeObserver(function() {
+  const width = canvas.clientWidth;
+  const height = canvas.clientHeight;
+  if (width <= 0 || height <= 0) return;
+  renderer.setSize(width, height, false);
+  camera.aspect = width / height;
+  camera.updateProjectionMatrix();
+});
+resizeObserver.observe(canvas);
+```
+
+For a custom WebGL renderer, implement equivalent canvas buffer/viewport and projection updates. Bound device pixel ratio for performance. Use requestAnimationFrame for actual rendering, and keep task state stable when the viewport reflows or the teacher/chat layout changes.
+
+Raycasting uses the canvas's current getBoundingClientRect and pointer client coordinates. Support touch pointer capture and safe cancellation. Keep models and destinations in view at narrow aspect ratios; adjust camera framing or scene layout without changing the objective or cue binding. Use visible picture buttons for equivalent model selection, and visible 48-pixel move/turn controls for small projected meshes or handles. Do not claim that the entire mesh hit area is 48 pixels when only the equivalent controls meet it.
+
+Use the responsive head marker, local CSS and actual 48-pixel controls from the shared contract. Tool groups wrap and content can scroll vertically. The model canvas must not grow over replay controls or the native classroom toolbar, teacher or discussion. Reduced motion removes nonessential spinning/animation while preserving intentional learner manipulation.
+
+## Opaque-origin dependency safety
+
+Do not assume that a same-host ES module URL is same-origin: this sandbox has an opaque origin. A module/dependency chain must be self-contained or actually CORS-accessible to that origin. Do not include an importmap pointing to unverified dependencies. If the frozen plan supplies a pinned self-contained module or encoded original module bytes, use that exact dependency and preserve the planned identity. Otherwise author complete geometry with dependencies that are actually supported; never invent a module URL or relax the sandbox.
+
+All imports in a dependency chain must resolve. A raw module containing imports cannot be made self-contained just by changing its top-level URL. A loading indicator is temporary; dependency failure presents the real retry path and cannot be reported as successful 3D.
+
+## Native states and teacher feedback
+
+Include one widget-config object with `type:"visualization3d"`, its visualizationType, exact objective, object identities, finite teaching states, allowed selection/transform/camera keys and stable target/control IDs. Implement all four native commands from the shared contract.
+
+SET_WIDGET_STATE validates finite state IDs, known object IDs, bounded finite position vectors and normalized nonzero orientation values. Route accepted transforms through the same object movement/placement/rotation functions used by child controls. Teacher demonstrations remain assisted, cannot bypass a task's feedback, and cannot assert independent completion. Camera changes update the real camera but do not count as object manipulation. Invalid state or target safely does nothing.
+
+Register every finite observe/select/move/turn/compare/mistake/hint/reset/retry/continue sentence in the single cue manifest. In every executable branch call the bridge directly with a declared literal cue ID; never pass state.cueId, a variable or a guarded function reference. Keep the current replay instruction synchronized with actual geometry and task phase.
+
+If the frozen design provides a real video for observation, it may be embedded with native pause/replay and a follow-up manipulation or picture action. Use the actual provided media URL, never a fake video card. Do not generate extra media or assess mastery.
+
+Return exactly one executable HTML document with genuine visible manipulable geometry, complete real handlers, finite teacher cues, responsive touch alternatives and the exact grade-bound content. Do not return an unfinished renderer or claim review/publication.
